    "writingTasks": {
      "wt-zj-2026-jan-application": {
        "writingTaskId": "wt-zj-2026-jan-application",
        "groupId": "group-zj-2026-jan-writing-application",
        "taskType": "application",
        "title": "应用文写作",
        "instruction": "你发现图书馆的书籍存在被勾画涂写的现象，请写一篇文章向校英文报投稿。内容包括：\n（1）说明具体情况；\n（2）呼吁爱护书籍。\n（1）写作词数应为80个左右；\n（2）请按如下格式在答题卡的相应位置作答。\nTreasure Library Books: Our Shared Responsibility",
        "wordCountTarget": 80
      },
      "wt-zj-2026-jan-continuation": {
        "writingTaskId": "wt-zj-2026-jan-continuation",
        "groupId": "group-zj-2026-jan-writing-continuation",
        "taskType": "continuation",
        "title": "读后续写",
        "instruction": "阅读下面材料，根据其内容和所给段落开头语续写两段，使之构成一篇完整的短文。\nI was driving my daughter Lila to her swimming lesson when we spotted a cow wandering down the main road. A thick rope was tied around its neck, dragging on the pavement behind it. The cow was ambling along in the middle of the traffic, tail flicking lazily, while a line of cars followed cautiously, their drivers leaning out of windows to stare. A group of kids on bicycles had gathered at a safe distance, cheering and pointing as the cow nudged a stop sign with its head.\n“Look, Mom!” Lila gasped, pressing her face against the car window. “City people are all scared of it! But you can handle anything, right? You herded cows on Grandpa’s farm when you were a kid.”\nI smiled, recalling the summer I’d spent on my grandfather’s dairy farm, chasing strays back into the pasture with a stick and a loud voice. “It’s just a lost cow, sweetie,” I said, “but we need to get you to swim class first. I’ll check on it after I drop you off.” We inched past the cow, which lifted its head and let out a low moo as we passed. Lila waved, and the cow tilted its head as if waving back. I dropped Lila at the pool, promising to be back in an hour, then turned the car around to find the cow. By the time I got back to the main road, the traffic had cleared, but the cow was still there – standing in a patch of grass by the sidewalk, chewing on a dandelion. It was a reddish-brown cow, smaller than the Holsteins on Grandpa’s farm, with big, gentle eyes. And there was something about it, a skittishness in its posture, that made it different from the cows I’d known back home. It didn’t seem like a farm cow at all.",
        "continuationStarters": [
          "As I turned to leave, two police officers pulled up in a patrol car.",
          "We tied the rope to a tree and discussed what to do with the cow."
        ],
        "materialText": "阅读下面材料，根据其内容和所给段落开头语续写两段，使之构成一篇完整的短文。\n\nI was driving my daughter Lila to her swimming lesson when we spotted a cow wandering down the main road. A thick rope was tied around its neck, dragging on the pavement behind it. The cow was ambling along in the middle of the traffic, tail flicking lazily, while a line of cars followed cautiously, their drivers leaning out of windows to stare. A group of kids on bicycles had gathered at a safe distance, cheering and pointing as the cow nudged a stop sign with its head.\n\n“Look, Mom!” Lila gasped, pressing her face against the car window. “City people are all scared of it! But you can handle anything, right? You herded cows on Grandpa’s farm when you were a kid.”\n\nI smiled, recalling the summer I’d spent on my grandfather’s dairy farm, chasing strays back into the pasture with a stick and a loud voice. “It’s just a lost cow, sweetie,” I said, “but we need to get you to swim class first. I’ll check on it after I drop you off.” We inched past the cow, which lifted its head and let out a low moo as we passed. Lila waved, and the cow tilted its head as if waving back. I dropped Lila at the pool, promising to be back in an hour, then turned the car around to find the cow. By the time I got back to the main road, the traffic had cleared, but the cow was still there – standing in a patch of grass by the sidewalk, chewing on a dandelion. It was a reddish-brown cow, smaller than the Holsteins on Grandpa’s farm, with big, gentle eyes. And there was something about it, a skittishness in its posture, that made it different from the cows I’d known back home. It didn’t seem like a farm cow at all.",
        "wordCountTarget": 150
      }
    }
  },
